<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John IV Laskaris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1250">1250</a>–c. 1305</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John IV Laskaris became emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> as a boy in 1258. His regent and co-emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1261 and then had John blinded and confined, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laskarid-dynasty">Laskarid dynasty</a>. The act caused the patriarch to excommunicate Michael and left lasting resentment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-iv-laskaris.jpg" alt="John IV Laskaris" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Byzantine scribes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:151_-_John_IV_Laskaris_(Mutinensis_-_color).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John IV Doukas Laskaris was born on 25 December 1250, a son of Theodore II Doukas Laskaris, the third emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, and Elena of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. His maternal grandparents were Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> of Bulgaria and his second wife Anna Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II of Hungary</a>. The Nicaean Empire was one of the Greek successor states formed after the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> by the Roman Catholics in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laskarid-dynasty">Laskarid dynasty</a> had done much to restore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>John was only seven when he inherited the throne on his father's death, and he reigned from 16 August 1258. His first regent was the bureaucrat George Mouzalon, but the nobility murdered Mouzalon, and their leader Michael Palaiologos took over the post. On 1 January 1259 Palaiologos made himself co-emperor as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII</a>; the two were second cousins once removed, both descended from Empress Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamatera.</p><p>Michael took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> on 25 July 1261, and John was left behind at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>. On his eleventh birthday, 25 December 1261, he was blinded on Michael's orders, which made him ineligible for the throne, and he was exiled and imprisoned in a fortress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>. The Patriarch Arsenius Autoreianus excommunicated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII</a> for the act, and a revolt led by a Pseudo-John IV later broke out near Nicaea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>John spent the rest of his life as a monk at Dacibyza. A rescript of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> dated 9 May 1273 refers to a report that John had escaped and invites him to Charles's court, and further documents attest a pension. This contradicts the historians George Pachymeres and Nikephoros Gregoras, who record that John stayed at Dacibyza until long after Michael's death; Deno John Geanakoplos concluded that the documents were propaganda meant to attract Greek supporters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laskarid-dynasty">Laskarid dynasty</a>. In 1290 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronikos II Palaiologos</a>, Michael's son and successor, visited John to seek forgiveness for the blinding. John died about 1305 and was eventually recognised as a saint, whose memory was revered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the 14th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John IV Doukas Laskaris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Michael VIII Palaiologos seized power from the boy emperor <b>John IV</b>, defeated the Latins and Epirotes at Pelagonia in 1259 and took Constantinople in July 1261, restoring the Byzantine Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in İznik, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.43,29.72&amp;year=1285" aria-label="İznik on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5280 719 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John IV Laskaris"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5730 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5730 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.43,29.72&amp;year=1285">What was here in 1285? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
